1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:17,856
This is STAT 331, video 13 b. Video 13 a wrote the least squares criterion: S of beta equals y transpose y, minus 2 y transpose X beta, plus beta transpose X transpose X beta.

2
00:00:18,306 --> 00:00:32,535
After this video, you can derive beta hat equals X transpose X inverse, X transpose y. You can show that the M L E is the same. And you can solve practice question 1 of Lecture 6.

3
00:00:32,985 --> 00:00:43,780
Take the derivative of S of beta with respect to the vector beta, one term at a time. y transpose y has no beta. Its derivative is 0.

4
00:00:44,230 --> 00:00:58,502
The term minus 2 y transpose X beta is a transpose beta, with a equal to minus 2 X transpose y. By the linear rule, its derivative is a, which is minus 2 X transpose y.

5
00:00:58,952 --> 00:01:14,227
The last term is a quadratic form in beta, with the matrix X transpose X. This matrix is symmetric, because its transpose is X transpose X again. So the derivative is 2 X transpose X beta.

6
00:01:14,677 --> 00:01:18,858
Add the three parts and set the sum equal to the zero vector.

7
00:01:19,308 --> 00:01:34,391
Move the first term to the right side and divide by 2. X transpose X, times beta, equals X transpose y. These are the normal equations. There are p plus 1 equations, one for each coefficient.

8
00:01:34,841 --> 00:01:50,457
Multiply both sides by the inverse of X transpose X. Beta hat equals X transpose X inverse, X transpose y. Learn this formula. It is the least squares estimate for every linear model in this course.

9
00:01:50,907 --> 00:02:07,632
The last step needs the inverse of X transpose X. The slide adds a condition: the columns of X must be linearly independent. Here is an example of what goes wrong. Suppose X has age in years and also age in months.

10
00:02:08,082 --> 00:02:28,519
Column 3 is 12 times column 2. Then X transpose X has no inverse. Many different beta give the same X beta, for example beta 1 equals 12 and beta 2 equals 0, or beta 1 equals 0 and beta 2 equals 1. The data cannot choose between them.

11
00:02:28,969 --> 00:02:38,249
If no column is a linear combination of the other columns, X transpose X is invertible, and there is exactly one beta hat.

12
00:02:38,699 --> 00:02:55,147
The normal equations have a picture. Move everything to one side. X transpose, times y minus X beta hat, is 0. y minus X beta hat is the residual vector e. So X transpose e equals 0.

13
00:02:55,597 --> 00:03:07,309
Think of y as one vector with n entries. The vectors X beta, for all beta, form a flat space. It is the column space of X. Here it is the purple plane.

14
00:03:07,759 --> 00:03:12,922
The fitted vector y hat equals X beta hat. It is in the column space.

15
00:03:13,372 --> 00:03:30,439
The residual e is y minus y hat. X transpose e equals 0 says that e is perpendicular to every column of X. So y hat is the point of the column space that is closest to y. That is why it minimizes S.

16
00:03:30,889 --> 00:03:47,763
The first column of X is all ones. So the sum of the residuals is 0. For our own data, the residuals are minus 0.2, minus 0.1, 1, minus 0.9, and 0.2. Their sum is 0.

17
00:03:48,213 --> 00:04:07,392
There is a second way to estimate beta: maximum likelihood. Under our assumptions, y is M V N with mean X beta and variance sigma squared I. The likelihood is the M V N density, with mu equal to X beta and Sigma equal to sigma squared I.

18
00:04:07,842 --> 00:04:20,215
We need two parts of the density. The determinant of a diagonal matrix is the product of its diagonal. So the determinant of sigma squared I is sigma squared to the power n.

19
00:04:20,665 --> 00:04:30,905
The inverse of a diagonal matrix inverts each diagonal entry. So the inverse of sigma squared I is 1 over sigma squared, times I.

20
00:04:31,355 --> 00:04:49,873
Take the log. The first term is minus n over 2 log 2 pi. The determinant gives minus n over 2 log sigma squared. The exponent gives minus 1 over 2 sigma squared, times y minus X beta transpose, y minus X beta.

21
00:04:50,323 --> 00:05:03,293
Look at the log-likelihood again. The last part is S of beta, the least squares criterion, times minus 1 over 2 sigma squared. The other two terms do not contain beta.

22
00:05:03,743 --> 00:05:16,693
So the log-likelihood is a constant minus a positive number times S of beta. The bottom curve is the log likelihood. It is the top curve S, turned upside down and moved.

23
00:05:17,143 --> 00:05:32,887
So the beta that makes S smallest makes the log-likelihood largest, for every value of sigma squared. The M L E of beta is the least squares estimate. M L E equals O L S, when all our assumptions hold.

24
00:05:33,337 --> 00:05:42,297
Note: the M L E needs normal errors. The least squares estimate does not. Least squares only minimizes the sum of squares.

25
00:05:42,747 --> 00:05:59,045
Here is practice question 1 of Lecture 6. With p equals 1, show that the matrix formula gives the simple linear regression estimates. With one covariate, X has two columns: ones, and the x i. Pause the video and try it.

26
00:05:59,495 --> 00:06:16,370
The goal is beta hat 1 equals S x y over S x x, and beta hat 0 equals y bar minus beta hat 1 x bar. The plan: compute X transpose X, invert it, and multiply by X transpose y.

27
00:06:16,820 --> 00:06:31,241
X transpose is 2 by n, and X is n by 2. So X transpose X is 2 by 2. Entry 1 1 is row 1 of X transpose times column 1 of X: the sum of n ones, which is n.

28
00:06:31,691 --> 00:06:44,513
Entry 1 2 is row 1 of X transpose times column 2 of X. It is the sum of 1 times x i, which is the sum of the x i. Entry 2 1 is the same sum.

29
00:06:44,963 --> 00:06:50,744
Entry 2 2 is row 2 times column 2. It is the sum of x i squared.

30
00:06:51,194 --> 00:06:59,045
X transpose y is 2 by 1. Its entries are the sum of the y i, and the sum of x i y i.

31
00:06:59,495 --> 00:07:05,425
X transpose X is symmetric. This agrees with the rule from the derivative slide.

32
00:07:05,875 --> 00:07:15,027
For a 2 by 2 matrix with entries a, b, c, d, the inverse is 1 over a d minus b c, times a new matrix.

33
00:07:15,477 --> 00:07:20,085
In the new matrix, swap a and d, and change the sign of b and c.

34
00:07:20,535 --> 00:07:35,042
Here a is n, b and c are the sum of x i, and d is the sum of x i squared. So a d minus b c is n times the sum of x i squared, minus the square of the sum of x i.

35
00:07:35,492 --> 00:07:49,167
Take out n. The sum of x i squared, minus n x bar squared, is S x x. So the determinant is n times S x x. It is positive when the x i are not all equal.

36
00:07:49,617 --> 00:08:00,049
So the inverse is 1 over n S x x, times the matrix: sum of x i squared, minus sum of x i, minus sum of x i, and n.

37
00:08:00,499 --> 00:08:14,600
Multiply the inverse by X transpose y. Row 2 gives n times the sum of x i y i, minus the sum of x i times the sum of y i. Row 1 gives the other entry.

38
00:08:15,050 --> 00:08:30,069
We use two identities. They come from the definitions of S x x and S x y, when you expand the squares and products. The sum of x i is n x bar, and the sum of y i is n y bar.

39
00:08:30,519 --> 00:08:44,599
For beta hat 1, write the sums as n x bar and n y bar. The numerator is n, times the sum of x i y i minus n x bar y bar. That bracket is S x y.

40
00:08:45,049 --> 00:08:53,305
The n cancels. So beta hat 1 equals S x y over S x x. This is the simple regression slope.

41
00:08:53,755 --> 00:09:09,072
Row 1 gives beta hat 0. Put in the identities. The sum of x i squared is S x x plus n x bar squared. The sum of x i y i is S x y plus n x bar y bar.

42
00:09:09,522 --> 00:09:15,922
Multiply out. The two terms n squared x bar squared y bar cancel. Take out n.

43
00:09:16,372 --> 00:09:33,012
Divide by n S x x. Beta hat 0 equals y bar minus x bar times S x y over S x x. That is y bar minus beta hat 1 x bar. Both estimates agree with simple linear regression.

44
00:09:33,462 --> 00:09:51,339
The picture shows the meaning. The fitted line goes through the point x bar, y bar. Go back x bar units to x equals 0. The line goes down by beta hat 1 times x bar. So the intercept is y bar minus beta hat 1 x bar.

45
00:09:51,789 --> 00:10:05,315
Check the result with our own small data set. n is 5. The sum of x is 15. The sum of x squared is 55. The sum of y is 20, and the sum of x y is 69.

46
00:10:05,765 --> 00:10:17,925
The determinant is 5 times 55, minus 15 squared, which is 50. x bar is 3, and S x x is 10. So n S x x is also 50.

47
00:10:18,375 --> 00:10:37,553
Multiply. Row 1 is 55 times 20, minus 15 times 69, which is 65. Row 2 is minus 15 times 20, plus 5 times 69, which is 45. Divide by 50. Beta hat is 1.3 and 0.9.

48
00:10:38,003 --> 00:10:53,854
The simple regression formulas give the same. S x y is 9, so beta hat 1 is 0.9. y bar is 4, so beta hat 0 is 4 minus 0.9 times 3, which is 1.3.

49
00:10:54,304 --> 00:10:59,211
The fitted line is y hat equals 1.3 plus 0.9 x.

50
00:10:59,661 --> 00:11:14,701
To recap. The model is y equals X beta plus epsilon. X has n rows and p plus 1 columns, with a first column of ones. Beta 0 is the mean outcome when all covariates are 0.

51
00:11:15,151 --> 00:11:23,876
Beta j is the difference in mean outcome for a one-unit change in covariate j, holding the other covariates fixed.

52
00:11:24,326 --> 00:11:39,281
Least squares minimizes S of beta. The derivative gives the normal equations, and beta hat equals X transpose X inverse, X transpose y. The columns of X must be linearly independent.

53
00:11:39,731 --> 00:11:55,475
With normal errors, the M L E is the same as least squares. With one covariate, the formula gives the simple regression estimates. The next video gives the properties of beta hat, the residuals, and sigma hat squared.
